<h2>The statutory map</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Conduct</th><th>Provision</th><th>Punishment range</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sexual harassment of a child — showing media, soliciting exhibition, repeated digital contact, threats using real or fabricated depictions</td><td>Sections 11–12, POCSO</td><td>Up to 3 years and fine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Using a child for pornographic purposes</td><td>Sections 13–14, POCSO</td><td>Minimum 5 years; higher on repetition or with assault</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage or possession of child sexual abuse material</td><td>Section 15, POCSO</td><td>Graded: fine to 5 years, by purpose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Publishing, transmitting or browsing such material; online enticement of children</td><td>Section 67B, IT Act</td><td>Up to 5 years and fine; 7 years on subsequent conviction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sexual assault using threats obtained online leading to contact offences</td><td>Sections 3–10, POCSO</td><td>As per the contact offence, including aggravated forms</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two features of this map matter in practice. First, the provisions overlap deliberately: a single course of online conduct — befriending, soliciting images, then threatening circulation — can attract Sections 11–12, 13–15 and 67B together, and chargesheets in Delhi commonly invoke the combination. Second, the POCSO offences are gender-neutral on both sides: the child may be a boy or a girl, and the accused may be of any gender.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of a grooming case</h2>
<p>Most online POCSO files follow a recognisable arc: contact on a platform where children spend time; a period of trust-building and normalisation; escalation to requests for images or calls; and then either continued exploitation under threat of circulation or an attempted move to a physical meeting. The legal significance of the arc is evidentiary — each stage leaves records. Friend requests, chat logs, payment trails for game credits or gifts, and the metadata of transmitted files together establish the sexual intent that Section 11 requires and rebut the defence of innocent association. For the prosecution, the sequencing also answers consent-based arguments before they begin: a child's apparent participation is legally irrelevant, since a minor's consent is no defence under the Act.</p>

<h2>Evidence: where these cases are won and lost</h2>
<p>Digital POCSO prosecutions stand on three evidentiary legs. The device and account forensics — seized phones, cloud data, platform records obtained through lawful process — must be proved with the certification the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam requires for electronic records. The child's statement, recorded with the safeguards POCSO mandates, supplies the narrative that the records corroborate. And the chain of custody for every device and export must hold, because the defence will probe alteration, shared access to the device, and the gap between the account and the accused — attribution being the classic battleground of cyber trials. Defence counsel, conversely, must test exactly these links; in no other POCSO category does the technical record dominate the outcome to the same degree.</p>

<h2>Reporting duties and the platform layer</h2>
<p>Section 19 POCSO casts the reporting obligation on any person — not only parents and professionals — and Section 21 makes non-reporting punishable for those who had knowledge. Schools, coaching platforms and gaming communities in Delhi increasingly operate written protocols for this reason. Platforms, for their part, generate automated reports of detected material that reach Indian investigators through established channels, and intermediaries face their own obligations under the IT framework. The practical consequence for families: early, documented reporting is both a legal duty and the single best step for an effective investigation.</p>

